module v1.compiler.realization_contract

import std.types { String, List }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }

// A v1-private compiler-realization declaration. Deliberately NOT v2's
// std.realization: importing it into the v1 seed risks a bootstrap inversion,
// and this row dissolves with v1 rather than outliving it.

type RealizationScope = TypecheckModuleScope

type MemoHitAdmission = RealizationProvesExactInputs

type MemoMissBehavior = EvaluateSubjectNormally

type RealizationRetention = ReleasedAtScopeExit

type PureMemoRealizationContract {
  subject: DeclarationRef
  scope: RealizationScope
  hit_admission: MemoHitAdmission
  miss_behavior: MemoMissBehavior
  retention: RealizationRetention
}

data pure_memo_realization_contracts: List<PureMemoRealizationContract> = [
  PureMemoRealizationContract {
    subject: decl_ref(module_path: "v1.compiler.infer_resolve", decl_name: "peel_nominal_alias_identity"),
    scope: TypecheckModuleScope,
    hit_admission: RealizationProvesExactInputs,
    miss_behavior: EvaluateSubjectNormally,
    retention: ReleasedAtScopeExit
  }
]

data pure_memo_realization_contract_note: String = "WHAT THIS DECLARES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT (operator ruling 2026-08-18). The contract states one thing: the subject denotes the SAME Node whether it is evaluated directly or through a memoized realization. That is the whole semantic content. It does not say how a realization proves that the input is unchanged, because that is a realization's business and differs per target: the Rust seed proves it with pointer identity over a retained strong reference, another target might use an intern identity or an object handle, and a target that simply evaluates every call is equally conforming. A conforming realization that never hits is CORRECT, merely slower, which is the property that makes this admissible at all.\n\nWHY THERE IS NO KEY, NO HIT/MISS VARIANT, AND NO THREADED STATE HERE. Writing the key into the model would assert that allocation identity IS semantic identity, which is false and is exactly the unsound-key class this repository has already been bitten by. Putting MemoHit or MemoMiss into a semantic result would make cache occupancy observable, at which point the realization is no longer transparent and correctness starts depending on cache availability. Threading a cache through the pure function to mirror one realization's implementation would import that realization's decomposition into the model, which is the fork this construction exists to avoid. Model and realization cannot answer the same modeled input differently; the pointer is implementation evidence that two runtime values are the same immutable object, not a meaning.\n\nWHY THIS IS ADMITTED under gunbc.v1_maintenance_standing: it adds no syntax, no type-system judgment, no newly accepted program, no compatibility obligation, no fallback arm and no persistent public surface. It is a behavior-preserving operational realization of a compiler-internal pure computation, against a measured repeated-work population on a live production compiler, and it dissolves when v1 leaves the production typecheck path.\n\nTHE BOUNDARY THE IMPLEMENTATION MUST RESPECT: regeneration must reproduce the optimization. A hand-written patch to a generated v1_compiler_*.rs would be erased by regen and is authority drift, so the realization lives in the emitter (v1 05_emit_rust) and the emitted runtime support (v1 runtime_rust), both of which regen produces from this authority."
